---
<Base title={article.title} description={article.description} type="article">
  <article class="narrow page-head prose">
    <span class="kicker">Reviews · Laptops</span>
    <h1>{article.title}</h1>
    <ArticleMeta article={article} />
<p class="lead">If you read my <a href={url('/articles/lenovo-legion-pro-7i-review/')}>Lenovo Legion Pro 7i review</a>, here are a few smaller-tier options. That laptop works well for what I do, but most people don't need an RTX 5080 for email, documents, browsers and video calls.</p>
<p>This post is for people who want something secure, fast and reliable for everyday basic work without paying gaming-laptop money.</p>
<p>One thing to be clear about: I haven't used these four machines myself. My picks are based on manufacturer specs and retailer listings, not hands-on testing. Prices are as of Oct 30, 2026, and they change often, so check the current price before you buy.</p>
<h2>The short list</h2>
<div class="table-scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="The short list (scrolls sideways on small screens)"><table class="vlan-table">
<thead><tr><th scope="col">Pick</th><th scope="col">Price (as of Oct 30, 2026)</th><th scope="col">Best for</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Apple Mac mini (M6)</td><td>From $899 at Apple</td><td>A quiet desk setup with your own monitors</td></tr>
<tr><td>Apple MacBook Air 13" (M5)</td><td>$1,299 at Apple (16GB/512GB)</td><td>A light laptop you carry every day</td></tr>
<tr><td>Lenovo ThinkPad E14 Gen 7</td><td>Check current price (Best Buy Marketplace seller)</td><td>Windows 11 Pro with room to upgrade RAM</td></tr>
<tr><td>HP ProBook 4 G1i 14"</td><td>Check current price (Best Buy Marketplace seller)</td><td>A Windows desk setup with up to three monitors</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<h2>Apple Mac mini (M6), from $899</h2>
<p><strong>Where to buy:</strong> <a data-umami-event="outbound-click" data-umami-event-item="apple.com" href="https://www.apple.com/shop/buy-mac/mac-mini">Apple</a></p>
<p>The Mac mini is the cheapest way on this list to get a modern, tightly integrated security platform. Apple silicon includes the Secure Enclave, and macOS has FileVault for full-disk encryption. It's small and quiet, and it drives external monitors.</p>
<p>The $899 figure is Apple's "from" price for the base M6 model (12-core CPU, 12-core GPU). Apple lists several memory and storage configurations, so check which one you're getting at that price.</p>
<p><strong>Who it suits:</strong> Someone who works at one desk and wants a quiet, low-fuss computer.</p>
<p><strong>Honest downside:</strong> It doesn't come with a screen, keyboard or mouse, so budget for those if you don't have them. It also can't run regular x86 Windows virtual machines. macOS virtualization only runs ARM-based guests, so if your work depends on Windows software in a VM, look at the Windows picks below.</p>
<h2>Apple MacBook Air 13" (M5), $1,299</h2>
<p><strong>Where to buy:</strong> <a data-umami-event="outbound-click" data-umami-event-item="apple.com" href="https://www.apple.com/shop/buy-mac/macbook-air">Apple</a></p>
<p>The $1,299 price is for the 13" M5 model with a 10-core CPU, 8-core GPU, 16GB of memory and 512GB of storage, per Apple's store page. The 15" model starts at $1,499. It's light and fanless, with long battery life. You get the same Secure Enclave and FileVault encryption as the Mac mini.</p>
<p><strong>Who it suits:</strong> Someone who carries a laptop every day and does mostly browser, document and video-call work.</p>
<p><strong>Honest downside:</strong> The memory can't be upgraded later, so buy the amount you'll need for the life of the machine. Support for external displays is also more limited than on a typical Windows docking setup. If you plan to use more than one external monitor, check Apple's current specs for the M5 Air before you buy.</p>
<h2>Lenovo ThinkPad E14 Gen 7</h2>
<p><strong>Where to buy:</strong> <a data-umami-event="outbound-click" data-umami-event-item="bestbuy.com" href="https://www.bestbuy.com/product/lenovo-thinkpad-e14-gen-7-21t9003pus-14-touchscreen-notebook-wuxga-60-hz-intel-core-ultra-7-240h-16-gb-512-gb-black/JJGSHYSV78/sku/13030553">Best Buy (Marketplace listing)</a>. Check the current price on the listing.</p>
<p>This configuration (model 21T9003PUS) has an Intel Core 7 240H, 16GB of RAM, a 512GB SSD and Windows 11 Pro. The listing title calls the chip a "Core Ultra 7 240H," and the listing's spec block has obvious errors in it, so confirm the exact chip and screen before you order. Based on reseller spec data, it has a 14" WUXGA touchscreen, a single 16GB DDR5 memory module that can be upgraded to 64GB, an IR camera with a privacy shutter, Wi-Fi 6E and a weight of about 2.9 lb.</p>
<p>Windows 11 Pro gives you BitLocker drive encryption and Hyper-V for virtual machines. ThinkPad is Lenovo's business line, and business lines usually come with less preinstalled software and longer BIOS support than consumer models.</p>
<p><strong>Who it suits:</strong> Someone who wants a Windows business laptop with room to grow.</p>
<p><strong>Honest downside:</strong> The screen is an entry-level 300-nit, 60 Hz panel, and the 48Wh battery is modest. The Best Buy listing is from a Marketplace seller, not Best Buy itself, so warranty and service can vary. Buy from Lenovo or an authorized seller if you can, and compare prices before you order.</p>
<h2>HP ProBook 4 G1i 14"</h2>
<p><strong>Where to buy:</strong> <a data-umami-event="outbound-click" data-umami-event-item="bestbuy.com" href="https://www.bestbuy.com/product/hp-probook-4-g1i-14-fhd-ai-notebook-intel-core-ultra-5-225u-with-16gb-memory-512gb-ssd-windows-11-pro-silver/J3GWT6QTZH/sku/13146468">Best Buy (Marketplace listing)</a>. Check the current price on the listing.</p>
<p>This configuration has an Intel Core Ultra 5 225U, 16GB of DDR5 memory, a 512GB SSD and Windows 11 Pro. According to the listing, it has a 14" 1920×1200 screen at 300 nits, supports up to three external monitors (HDMI 2.1 plus two USB-C ports with DisplayPort 1.4), and includes wired Ethernet, Wi-Fi 6E, a webcam privacy shutter and HP Wolf Security.</p>
<p><strong>Who it suits:</strong> Someone who wants a lower-cost business laptop and works at a desk with multiple monitors.</p>
<p><strong>Honest downside:</strong> The low-power U-series chip and basic display are fine for office work and maybe a light VM, but not heavy virtualization. Like the ThinkPad, this listing is from a Marketplace seller, so warranty and service can vary. Check the current price before you buy.</p>
<h2>What to look for in a secure, reliable work machine</h2>
<p>You don't need anything exotic for everyday work. These basics cover most of it:</p>
<ul class="checklist">
<li><strong>Windows 11 Pro</strong> (for BitLocker encryption and Hyper-V), or <strong>macOS</strong> with FileVault turned on</li>
<li><strong>TPM 2.0 and Secure Boot</strong> support on Windows, plus a BIOS password option (Macs use Apple's Secure Enclave)</li>
<li><strong>Fingerprint or face login</strong> if you want to skip typing a password. On Windows, look for a fingerprint reader or an IR camera for Windows Hello</li>
<li><strong>A webcam privacy shutter</strong></li>
<li><strong>16GB of RAM minimum</strong>, or 32GB+ if you'll run VMs. Upgradeable slots are a plus</li>
<li><strong>VT-x/VT-d</strong> (Intel) or <strong>AMD-V/IOMMU</strong> if you need virtualization</li>
<li><strong>A 512GB+ SSD</strong></li>
<li><strong>Enough video outputs</strong> for the number of monitors you use</li>
<li><strong>Ongoing update support.</strong> Check that the maker publishes BIOS and driver updates for your exact model. Business lines (ThinkPad, EliteBook, ProBook, Dell Pro) usually get longer BIOS support</li>
<li><strong>Buy from the manufacturer or an authorized retailer</strong> when you can, for a clean factory install and a real warranty</li>
</ul>
<h2>Which one I'd look at first</h2>
<p>If you work at one desk and don't need Windows, the Mac mini is the least expensive way in. If you carry your computer every day, look at the MacBook Air. If you need Windows, the ThinkPad gives you more room to upgrade, and the ProBook is built for a multi-monitor desk. If your work regularly involves heavy virtual machines or GPU work, none of these will replace a machine like the one in my <a href={url('/articles/lenovo-legion-pro-7i-review/')}>Legion Pro 7i review</a>.</p>
<p><em>— Michael Ream</em></p>
